id	speaker	text	about
rep01	igor_suhorukov	I managed to plug ClickHouse's MergeTree table engine into PostgreSQL as a table access method	{ch_table_engines,mergetree,pg_vexec,table_access_method}
rep02	architect_friend	Why? Just take ClickHouse and don't torture yourself.	{clickhouse}
rep03	igor_suhorukov	Because I need JOINs across many tables, consistency of data in the system, and one platform that is familiar to develop and maintain.	{claim_use_case,postgresql}
rep04	architect_friend	No such thing. Either reliable or fast!	{claim_reliable_or_fast}
rep05	igor_suhorukov	And I managed to combine the best of both worlds.	{claim_final_result}
rep06	architect_friend	But you recently ported Greenplum into an extension for postgres and then wrote a vector executor on top of it. Why do you need MergeTree?	{ev_cloudberry_port,mergetree,pg_vexec}
rep07	igor_suhorukov	Vectorization grinds through columns fast but chokes at the moment data is read.	{claim_bottleneck_reading,vectorized_execution}
rep08	architect_friend	18%, seriously? And was that worth building vectorization for?	{obs_cb_orca_heap_vexec,vectorized_execution}
rep09	igor_suhorukov	Well, I ran profiling and saw that function kernels took only 0-2% of the total query execution time.	{claim_kernels_negligible,obs_kernel_share,obs_page_copy_share,obs_wide_rows_share}
rep10	architect_friend	Then take a columnar storage. There are a dozen of them for PostgreSQL.	{columnar_storage}
rep11	igor_suhorukov	Well, aren't you a fine adviser, Captain Obvious) On unmodified PostgreSQL19, vexec did not find a single source that would hand out data column by column.	{aoco,citus_columnar,claim_no_columnar_source,hydra_columnar,obs_port_gp_ao,pax}
rep12	architect_friend	So you just linked ClickHouse with the postgres backend?	{ch_table_engines,clickhouse}
rep13	igor_suhorukov	Not just that. MergeTree does not build on its own; it is part of the ClickHouse DBMS and drags along the interpreter, parsers and functions.	{claim_custom_glue,libchte_engine,mergetree,obs_submodules}
rep14	architect_friend	Fine, analytics. But admit that postgres on data updates with long-running transactions is a pain.	{bloat,claim_friend_bloat,claim_heap_update_pain}
rep15	igor_suhorukov	I admit it! That's why I made my own iheap	{iheap,inplace_update,undo_log}
rep16	architect_friend	All right. And why all this if ClickHouse is faster anyway?	{clickhouse,obs_final_ch}
rep17	igor_suhorukov	Because it is an excellent database for a huge metrics table with hundreds of columns.	{claim_specialized_complexity,claim_use_case}
