My PostgreSQL is faster than yours and has almost caught up with ClickHouse	In a dispute with an architect friend who runs four different DBMSs in production, the author tells how, without a single core patch, he built ClickHouse's MergeTree table engine into PostgreSQL 19 (ch_table_engines), connected his vectorized executor pg_vexec to it and made the table access method iheap with in-place row updates. Vectorization on heap gave only 18–19%: the bottleneck turned out to be reading rows, not computation; with MergeTree and a series of optimizations the geometric mean ClickBench time fell from 715 to 49.9 ms, only 3 times slower than ClickHouse on the same machine. The author's conclusion: for projects not at big-tech scale, one PostgreSQL DBMS with extensions is simpler than a zoo of specialized databases.	{clickbench,clickhouse,mergetree,pg_vexec,postgresql,table-access-method,tableAM,vectorization}	{ch_table_engines,iheap,mergetree,pg_vexec,postgresql}
