habr-1097022	My PostgreSQL is faster than yours and has almost caught up with ClickHouse	In a dispute with an architect friend who runs four different DBMSs in production, the author tells how, without a single core patch, he built ClickHouse's MergeTree table engine into PostgreSQL 19 (ch_table_engines), connected his vectorized executor pg_vexec to it and made the table access method iheap with in-place row updates. Vectorization on heap gave only 18–19%: the bottleneck turned out to be reading rows, not computation; with MergeTree and a series of optimizations the geometric mean ClickBench time fell from 715 to 49.9 ms, only 3 times slower than ClickHouse on the same machine. The author's conclusion: for projects not at big-tech scale, one PostgreSQL DBMS with extensions is simpler than a zoo of specialized databases.	{schema:Article,schema:BlogPosting,schema:CreativeWork,schema:SocialMediaPosting,schema:TechArticle}	article	{"polemical dialogue","performance study","personal experience","development overview"}	{clickbench,clickhouse,mergetree,pg_vexec,postgresql,table-access-method,tableAM,vectorization}	{ch_table_engines,iheap,mergetree,pg_vexec,postgresql}	{architect_friend,igor_suhorukov}	igor_suhorukov	2026-10-21 06:00:36	ru	1097022	https://habr.com/ru/articles/1097022/	10	4234	9	31	8	8	0	habr	Habr	https://habr.com/	igor_suhorukov	179	331	habr/1097022.md	text/markdown	19690	12138	61c608f8417e8b389ddd9a265a0fa01d9c07bdf71cb3df9831d0252e2cbc4ac5	2026-10-26 17:24:45+00	extract/habr-1097022.yaml	search by names and aliases with lemmatization (pymorphy3), without coreference resolution	third	first-person pronouns outside the replies: 7.4 per 1000 words	1	2.13	7.32	70e146e0e426	1511	106	41	14.25	13.5	36.9	0.58	0.64	0.315	7	22	1	1	0.286	2	{claim_bottleneck_reading,claim_custom_glue,claim_heap_update_pain,claim_need_columnar,claim_not_all_steps,claim_postgres_can_compete}
